ased on volume, so transporting less would mean lower costs. That makes sense. So D seems to be the correct answer.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The context says wood chips are less than half the volume, so if you make chips at the logging site, you're moving less volume to the factory. That would reduce transportation costs because you're not moving as much. So D is supported by the info given.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is D.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding the Context:**\n   - United Lumber uses trees for lumber and wood chips.\n   - Conversion (debranching and chipping) costs are the same at both the logging site and factory.\n   - Wood chips take up less than half the volume of the branches.\n\n2. **Analyzing the Options:**\n   - **A:** Not supported by context; no mention of transportation logistics.\n   - **B:** Incorrect because cost isn't an advantage; it's the same.\n   - **C:** Incorrect as cost isn't mentioned to be more economical at the factory.\n   - **D:** Correct because transporting less volume (wood chips) from logging site reduces costs.\n\n**Answer:** D",
