he administrative department's reasoning about matching the number of buses to the number of students and teachers.\n\nOption D: The education department has no plan to expand the scale of teachers and students. This is about not expanding, which would mean the school size isn't going to grow. If they don't plan to expand, then the number of buses needed won't increase. This also seems relevant because the administrative department's decision was based on the current number, implying that they don't expect growth.\n\nNow, I need to decide between C and D. Both seem relevant, but which one is better.\n\nThe context says the existing buses are enough based on the current number and scale. So if the number of students isn't going to increase (C), then the current buses are sufficient. Similarly, if the department doesn't plan to expand (D), the school size won't grow, so the buses are enough.\n\nBut the question is about the assumption that best supports the decision. The decision was to reject the application because the number of buses matches the school size and number of people. So the assumption would be that the number of people (students and teachers) isn't going to increase, which is C. Alternatively, if the department doesn't plan to expand, that would also mean the number won't grow, which is D.\n\nWait, but the context says the department rejected the application because the number of buses must match the size and number of teachers and students. So the assumption is that the number of teachers and students is fixed, or at least not increasing. So both C and D could support that.\n\nBut looking at the options, C is about the number of children not increasing, which directly affects the number of students needing buses. D is about not expanding the scale, which could mean teachers and students, but it's a bit more general.\n\nI think C is more specific to the transportation needs, so it's the best assumption to support the decision.\n</think>\n\nThe best assumption to support the decision is that the number of primary school-age children in the area will not increase much in the future.\n\n**Answer: C**",
