      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option supports the meteorologist's argument the most. Let me read the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe meteorologist says that the number of tornadoes reported annually has more than doubled since the 1950s, but the actual number hasn't increased. The reason given is that our ability to detect tornadoes has improved, so we're just finding a higher percentage of them than before.\n\nSo, the key point here is that detection methods have improved, leading to more reported tornadoes, but the actual number hasn't gone up. That means the increase is due to better detection, not more tornadoes.\n\nNow, looking at the question: which option, if true, supports this argument the most? The argument is that the number reported has more than doubled, but actual number hasn't increased because detection is better.\n\nLet's go through the options one by one.\n\nOption A: Annual deaths have increased steadily since the 1950s. Hmm, if more tornadoes are reported, but the actual number hasn't increased, does that mean more deaths? Not necessarily. It depends on the intensity of the tornadoes. If we're detecting more tornadoes, but they're not necessarily stronger, the number of deaths might not increase. So this might not support the argument directly.\n\nOption B: The number hitting major population centers has more than doubled. This seems relevant because if more tornadoes are detected, especially in areas where they can cause more damage, that would fit with the idea that we're finding more. So this supports the argument that more are being reported because detection is better.\n\nOption C: The geographic range where tornadoes are most prevalent has remained constant. If the range hasn't changed, that might mean that we're not detecting tornadoes in new areas, which could contradict the idea that detection has improved. Or maybe it's just that the same areas are more efficiently monitored. I'm not sure if this supports the argument as much as B.\n\nOption D: The number of large and medium tornadoes reported has remained constant. Wait, the context says that the actual number hasn't increased, but the reported number has more than doubled. So if large and medium tornadoes are reported the same, that might not fit because the total reported has gone up, but maybe the size distribution has changed. So this might not support the argument as much as B.\n\nPutting it all together, the argument is about detection improvements leading to more reported tornadoes without an actual increase. Option B specifically mentions more tornadoes hitting major centers, which ties into detection improvements because those areas are more likely to be monitored closely. So B supports the argument the most.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **B**. \n\n**Explanation:**\nThe meteorologist's argument hinges on improved detection methods leading to a higher reported number of tornadoes without an actual increase in their occurrence. Option B highlights that tornadoes hitting major population centers have more than doubled, which aligns with better detection capabilities in densely populated areas. This directly supports the idea that more tornadoes are being reported due to improved detection rather than an actual rise in their frequency. \n\n**Answer: B**",
