g and cause chronic diseases. The original context only mentions asthma. So C is extending the original statement by adding more effects of AGEs, which the context doesn't mention. So this would question the original statement's scope.\n\nOption D: AGEs are natural proteins in the body and accumulate with age. The context doesn't mention AGEs being natural or their accumulation with age. It just talks about them being released from meat. So D is introducing new information not in the context, which could question the original statement.\n\nNow, the question is which option most questions the original statement. The original statement is that AGEs from meat cause asthma in kids, so the question is about the validity of this link.\n\nOption A talks about whether AGEs from non-grilled meat cause asthma. The original says grilled meat causes it, so A is about a different scenario, but it's a related point.\n\nOption B is about where AGEs come from in the body. The original only mentions meat as a source when grilled, so B is questioning if that's the main source.\n\nOption C is about other effects of AGEs beyond asthma, which the original doesn't mention, so it's a broader question.\n\nOption D is about the natural occurrence of AGEs, which the original doesn't address, so it's another point of question.\n\nBut the question is which option most questions the original statement. The original is about meat causing asthma via AGEs. So options that directly address this link would be more relevant.\n\nOption A is about whether non-grilled meat causes it, which is a different point but related.\n\nOption B is about the main source of AGEs, which is broader but could affect the original statement's validity.\n\nOption C and D are about other effects, which are beyond the original statement.\n\nSo, the options that most question the original statement are A, B, and maybe C and D, but the most direct would be A and B.\n\nWait, the question is which option, if true, most questio