      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out the correct answer to this logical reasoning question. Let me read the context carefully and then the question again.\n\nThe context says that my country's economy is slowing down because of macroeconomic policies, which will reduce the demand for oil and natural gas. The International Energy Agency (IEA) predicts that this year, my country's crude oil production will reach 175 million tons, a 1% increase from last year. However, crude oil consumption is expected to exceed 300 million tons, a 12% increase from last year. Imports are also expected to exceed 100 million tons, possibly close to 120 million tons, which is a 30% increase from last year.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option can be inferred from this text. The options are A, B, C, or D.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: My country's crude oil supply is tight. Hmm, the text mentions that production is increasing. It says production will reach 175 million tons, which is a 1% increase. So, supply is actually going up, not tight. So A is probably incorrect.\n\nOption B: My country's crude oil consumption mainly depends on imports. The text says that consumption is expected to exceed 300 million tons, and imports are expected to exceed 100 million tons, possibly up to 120 million. So, if imports are a significant part of the consumption, then yes, consumption depends mainly on imports. But wait, the text doesn't explicitly say that all or most of the consumption is from imports. It just says imports are increasing. So maybe B is not necessarily correct because it's possible that some is produced domestically. But the text does say that production is 175 million, which is less than the consumption of 300 million. So the difference must be made up by imports. So, the consumption is more than what's produced, so imports must be the main source. Therefore, B seems correct.\n\nOption C: My country's dependence on imported crude oil 