      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this question about public products and pure public products. Let me try to figure it out step by step. \n\nFirst, the context says that public products are provided by the public sector to meet public needs. There are two types mentioned: pure public products and other types. Pure public products are defined as consumption by any one person without reducing the consumption of others. So, the key here is that one person's use doesn't affect others.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) National defense. Hmm, national defense is something the government provides, like the military or protection. But does it fit as a pure public product? I think national defense is more of a public good because it's a collective good that everyone benefits from, but it's not something you consume individually. It's more about security for the whole society. So maybe it's not a pure public product but a public good.\n\nB) Expressway tolls due to congestion. Tolls are charges for using a road. If it's due to congestion, maybe it's meant to manage traffic. But tolls are a form of pricing, so it's more like a user fee. It's provided by the public sector, but it's not free. So people pay to use it, which means it's not a pure public product because using it reduces the consumption of others (they have to pay, so it's not free for all).\n\nC) Free parks built in the city. Parks are a place where people can go and enjoy without paying. If they're free, then anyone can use them without it affecting others. So one person's use doesn't reduce another's. That fits the definition of a pure public product because it's provided freely to the public.\n\nD) Free gifts provided by the mall. Malls might have promotions where they give gifts, but these are usually limited and not a regular public service. It's more of a marketing strategy. Also, gifts are not a consistent public product, so it doesn't fit the definition.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the pure public 