er risk by other insurers. If other companies rejected them, maybe they have more health issues or higher claims potential. That could actually increase the company's losses, so this doesn't seem like a good strategy to minimize losses. It might be the opposite.\n\nOption D: Attracting middle-aged customers unlikely to submit claims for many years. Middle-aged people are younger than the elderly, so they might be healthier on average. If they're middle-aged, they might not need the policy as much yet, or they might not file claims for several years. This could mean fewer claims in the short term, giving the company time to assess the policy without immediate high costs. Also, since the policy is for elderly people, targeting those who are middle-aged now might mean they'll age into the policy later, but the strategy is about minimizing losses now. So attracting middle-aged people who are less likely to claim benefits for several years could help reduce the immediate claims pressure, which is what the company is concerned about.\n\nWait, but the policy is specifically for elderly people. So maybe the company is targeting the elderly directly. But if they can't get enough income from premiums, they need to reduce claims. So maybe the best way is to limit the number of people who can claim, or the amount they can claim.\n\nOption D suggests attracting middle-aged customers, who might not need the policy yet. But the policy is for elderly people, so maybe the company is trying to cover them. Alternatively, if they target middle-aged people, perhaps those who are less likely to need the policy's services for many years, thus reducing the number of claims in the near term.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the company should limit the coverage or set higher deductibles, but those options aren't listed here. So among the given options, D seems like the best fit because it's about attracting people who are less likely to make claims, thus reducing the potential losses.\n\nSo I think 