aterials such as DNA.\" The context mentions that under certain conditions, the half-life is shorter. So environmental factors do influence DNA decay. But the scientist's point is based on the half-life being about 158,000 years. If environmental factors can change the decay rate, maybe in some cases it's faster or slower. But the study already considered certain conditions, so if environmental factors can make it decay faster, that might mean that the DNA doesn't last as long as the study suggests. Wait, but the study says that under certain conditions, the half-life is 158,000 years. So if environmental factors can make it decay faster, that would mean the actual half-life could be less, which would support the idea that DNA doesn't last long enough. But the scientist is using the half-life to argue against the possibility of reconstructing dinosaurs. So if environmental factors can slow down decay, maybe DNA could last longer, which would make the scientist's point weaker. Hmm, this is a bit confusing. Let me think again. The scientist says that the half-life is about 158,000 years, so DNA doesn't last long enough. If environmental factors can affect the decay rate, maybe in some cases it's slower, meaning DNA could last longer than 158,000 years. That would mean the scientist's point is incorrect because DNA could indeed last long enough for reconstruction. So C could refute the scientist's point by showing that DNA might last longer than the study suggests.\n\nOption D: \"The sequence of base pairs of Dinornis and dinosaur is different.\" Dinornis is a type of dinosaur, so if their DNA sequences are different, that would mean that even if we had DNA, we couldn't reconstruct the exact dinosaur. But the scientist's point is about the half-life, not about the sequence differences. So D is about the feasibility of reconstructing the specific species, not the time frame. So D doesn't directly address the scientist's main argument about the half-life.\n\nSo, to recap