efore, D is not the assumption.\n\nWait, the argument is that the climate is cooler now, so the trees can grow higher. The assumption must be that if the climate were warmer, trees wouldn't grow as high. So the assumption is that the climate was warmer 10,000 years ago, allowing trees to grow where they couldn't now. So the assumption is that the climate was warmer back then, making B correct because it says the climate hasn't been significantly warmer since then.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the assumption is that the current cooler climate allows trees to grow higher, so the past was warmer. So the argument relies on the assumption that the climate was warmer 10,000 years ago, which is what B is saying.\n\nSo I think the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe argument relies on the assumption that the Rocky Mountains have never been significantly warmer than during the lifetime of the trees whose fossilized remains have been found. This assumption is crucial because if the climate were warmer in the past, trees might not have been able to grow as high as they did 10,000 years ago.\n\n**Answer: B**",
