context mentions that experts are suggesting the decline isn't related to tree cutting, so both B and D could support that. But the question is about the best option.\n\nLooking back, the context says the decline is often attributed to tree cutting, but experts say it's not necessarily related. So the best support would be evidence that tree cutting didn't cause the decline. Option B shows that even after the last tree was cut, people and farming were fine. That directly contradicts the original belief, so it supports the experts' view.\n\nOption D is about a different cause (colonization), which also supports the idea that other factors caused the decline, but the question is specifically about the tree cutting theory. So B is more directly relevant.\n</think>\n\nThe best option that supports the experts' view is:\n\n**B) Archaeological findings show that after the last tree (palm tree) on the island was felled, there were still a large number of indigenous people living, and the level of agricultural farming did not decline.**\n\nThis option directly contradicts the original belief that tree cutting led to decline, thus supporting the experts' alternative explanation.",
