      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me read through it carefully.\n\nThe context is about a Beijing newspaper article titled \"15% of fathers raise children for others.\" The article references statistics from a judicial material evidence appraisal center. They say that out of nearly 600 people who did paternity tests in Beijing within a year, 15% of the test results excluded paternity. So, that means 15% of the tests showed that the man wasn't the biological father.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options does NOT question the reliability of the statistical inference. The options are A, B, C, or D.\n\nFirst, I need to understand what the context is saying. The newspaper is reporting that 15% of paternity tests in Beijing excluded paternity. So, the statistical inference here is that 15% of fathers in Beijing are not the biological fathers of their children. The question is about which option doesn't question this reliability.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: The title of the article should be limited: 15% of the fathers who carry out paternity tests raise children for others.\n\nHmm, the current title is \"15% of fathers raise children for others.\" The option suggests that the title should be limited to fathers who conducted paternity tests. So, the original title might be misleading because it's not specifying that these fathers are the ones who did the tests. This is more about the sample group. So, this is questioning the title's accuracy, implying that the title doesn't clearly state the sample, which could affect the reliability of the inference. So, this does question the reliability.\n\nOption B: When the paternity test is conducted, there are already doubts about the paternity relationship.\n\nThis option is about the context in which the tests are conducted. If people are getting paternity tests because they have doubts, that might affect the sample's representativeness.