f the second drawer has a book, C's statement that there's no pen there could still be true or false. Wait, no, C says there is no pen in the second drawer. If the second drawer has a book, it might or might not have a pen. So C could be true or false. Similarly, D says there are letters in the third drawer. If all drawers have books, the third drawer has a book, which may or may not have letters. So D could be true or false.\n\nBut since only B is true, and the others must be false. So A is false, meaning it's not the case that some drawers have no books. So all have books. So A is false. Now, for C to be false, C must be false. So C says there's no pen in the second drawer. If C is false, then there is a pen in the second drawer. But wait, the second drawer has a book. So can it have both a book and a pen? The problem doesn't specify that drawers can't have multiple items. So if the second drawer has a book and a pen, then C is false (because there is a pen), and D says there are letters in the third drawer. If the third drawer has a book, maybe it's a letter, but again, not specified. So D could be true or false.\n\nBut wait, if only B is true, then A, C, D must be false. So C must be false, meaning there is a pen in the second drawer. So in this case, option A (pen in second drawer) is true.\n\nSimilarly, D must be false, so there are no letters in the third drawer. So option B (letters in third drawer) is false.\n\nSo in this case, option A is true.\n\nCase 3: Only C is true.\n\nC says there is no pen in the second drawer. So the second drawer has no pen.\n\nIf only C is true, then A, B, D must be false.\n\nA is false, so it's not the case that some drawers have no books. So all drawers have books. Wait, but if all drawers have books, then B would be true, but B is false. So this is a contradiction. Because if all drawers have books, B is true, but in this case, only C is true. Therefore, this scenario is impossible. So C cannot be the only one true because that would make B also true, which contradicts the condition that only one is true.\n\nSo Case 3 is invalid.\n\nCase 4: Only D is true.\n\nD says there are letters in the third drawer.\n\nIf only D is true, then A, B, C must be false.\n\nA is false, so it's not the case that some drawers have no books. So all drawers have books. So B would be true, but B is false. Contradiction again. So D cannot be the only one true because that would make B also true, which is not allowed.\n\nSo Cases 3 and 4 are invalid because they lead to contradictions.\n\nTherefore, the only possible cases are Case 1 (A true) and Case 2 (B true). Wait, but in Case 2, when B is true, we saw that C must be false, so there is a pen in the second drawer, and D must be false, so no letters in the third drawer. So in both cases, when only A or only B is true, option A (pen in second drawer) is true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check.\n\nIn Case 1: Only A is true.\n\n- A: No books in some drawers (true)\n- B: All drawers have books (false)\n- C: No pen in second drawer (false) \u2192 pen in second drawer (so A is true)\n- D: Letters in third drawer (false) \u2192 no letters in third drawer.\n\nSo in this case, option A is true.\n\nIn Case 2: Only B is true.\n\n- B: All drawers have books (true)\n- A: No books in some drawers (false) \u2192 all have books\n- C: No pen in second drawer (false) \u2192 pen in second drawer\n- D: Letters in third drawer (false) \u2192 no letters in third drawer.\n\nSo again, option A is true.\n\nSo in both possible valid cases, option A is true. Therefore, regardless of whether only A or only B is true, option A must be true.\n\nWait, but let me check the options again.\n\nThe options are:\n\nA) There is a pen in the second drawer.\n\nB) There are letters in the third drawer.\n\nC) There are books in all four drawers.\n\nD) There are no books in the four drawers.\n\nSo in both cases, option A is true. So the answer must be A.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure I'm not missing any