een space near his fence is his. So, he's making a mistake by assuming ownership based on proximity or community membership without proper evidence.\n\nLooking at the options, D seems similar because it's taking a group's property and attributing it to an individual without justification. But C is also a form of logical fallacy, assuming that because one can't read all, a specific one must be his. But Xiao Li's mistake is about ownership, so maybe D is more similar because it's about group property and individual ownership.\n\nWait, but in the context, Xiao Li is arguing that the green space near his fence is his because he's part of the community. So, he's conflating community membership with ownership of specific property. Similarly, in D, the king Yiya is part of the Mobi Knights, so Yiya is invincible. It's assuming that because Yiya is part of the group, she has the same properties as the group.\n\nSo, both C and D involve logical fallacies, but which one is more similar to Xiao Li's mistake? Xiao Li's mistake is about ownership based on community membership, so D is about group properties applying to an individual. That seems similar.\n\nBut wait, C is about a specific work being Dumas's, while D is about an individual being invincible because part of a group. So, both involve incorrect assumptions about ownership or properties.\n\nHmm, maybe C is more about a specific item, while D is about a person's attributes. Xiao Li's mistake is about ownership, so maybe C is about ownership as well. But C is about reading all works, so it's more about generalization from lack of information.\n\nWait, the question is about which option is most similar to Xiao Li's mistake. Xiao Li's mistake is that he assumes ownership based on community membership, so the green space near his fence is his. So, he's conflating community membership with ownership of a specific area.\n\nLooking at the options, D is about a group's property (army) and an individual's property (king Yiya). S