      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me read through it carefully.\n\nThe context is about government insurance for bank deposits. The government requires banks to pay the premiums for this insurance. The argument is that since depositors are the ones who primarily benefit from the security, the government should make them bear the cost instead of the banks. So, the government is shifting the responsibility from the banks to the depositors.\n\nNow, the question is asking which principle, if established, would best justify drawing the conclusion based on the reasons given. The options are A, B, C, or D.\n\nLet me break down each option to see which one aligns best with the argument.\n\nOption A: Any rational system of insurance must base the size of premiums on the degree of risk involved. Hmm, this seems related to how insurance works in general. Typically, higher risk means higher premiums. But the argument here isn't about the size of the premiums but who should pay them. So, while A is a true statement about insurance, it doesn't directly address who bears the cost, which is the main point of the argument.\n\nOption B: The people who stand to benefit from an economic service should always be made to bear the costs of that service. This seems to directly support the argument. The depositors benefit from the insurance, so they should pay for it. This principle is about cost-bearing based on benefit, which is exactly what the argument is advocating for.\n\nOption C: The choice of not accepting an offered service should always be available, even if there is no charge for the service. This is about choice and availability. The argument doesn't mention anything about choice; it's more about who should pay. So, C doesn't seem relevant here.\n\nOption D: Government-backed security for investors, such as bank depositors, should be provided only when it does not reduce incentives for investors to make responsible investments.