d and value reading, which would lead to more reading. But how does that explain the decrease in the number of books? Maybe because they're reading longer books, so the count is lower but the actual reading is more.\n\nOption C suggests that children are used to listening, which isn't counted. If they're listening, maybe they're not reading as much, but the context says the number of books read increased, so that might not fit.\n\nOption D suggests electronic reading isn't counted. If parents are reading electronically, maybe the count of physical books is lower, but the context says the number of books increased, so maybe that's not it.\n\nOption A suggests children have too much schoolwork, so they don't read as much. But the context says parents are reading more with them, so maybe the parents are reading more, but the children's own reading is less. So if the children are too busy, they might not be reading as much, but the parents are compensating by reading with them, leading to more books being read by the family as a whole.\n\nWait, but the total number of books read by children went up, but per person it went down. So maybe the number of children in the families increased, but the per person count went down. Or perhaps the books are longer, so each book counts as more pages, but the number of books is less.\n\nSo, considering all this, the best explanation might be that the parents are more educated and value reading, so they read more with their children, leading to more books being read, but the children's per person count went down because the books are longer. So option B seems plausible.\n\nAlternatively, option C could be a factor if the children are used to listening, but the context says the number of books read increased, so maybe that's not it.\n\nWait, the context says that the number of books read by parents and children increased, but the length of the books also increased. So the total number of books read is up, but the length per book is up,