irectly supports the executor's decision to sell the farm because it was necessary to clear the debts. If this is true, it means the executor had no choice but to sell it, which aligns with the solicitor's argument that Loux wouldn't have objected. So this might actually strengthen the argument, not weaken it.\n\nOption C: Loux was well aware of the legal requirements the executor would have to satisfy. This means she knew that the executor had to make decisions like selling properties to clear debts. If she was aware, she might have expected the executor to make such decisions, which means she wouldn't object to selling the farm. This supports the solicitor's argument, so it doesn't weaken it.\n\nOption D: Loux's grandson had repeatedly expressed his desire to own a farm. If the grandson wanted a farm, especially the Stoke Farm, this could mean that Loux might have wanted to transfer it to him. If the executor didn't transfer it, Loux might have been upset or objected. Therefore, if the grandson wanted it, this could go against the executor's decision to sell it, which would weaken the solicitor's argument that Loux wouldn't object.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The solicitor's argument is that Loux didn't express any particular desire about the farm, implying she didn't care if it was sold or not. If the grandson wanted it, that could mean Loux might have wanted it transferred, so the executor's decision not to transfer it could have been against her wishes. Therefore, this information would show that Loux might have had an objection, which weakens the solicitor's argument.\n\nSo, the options that weaken the argument are A, B, C, or D. Let me reevaluate each.\n\nOption A: If the farm was the main cause of debts, selling it was necessary, which the executor did, so this supports the argument, not weakens it.\n\nOption B: Same as A, it supports the executor's decision, so strengthens the argument.\n\nOption C: If Loux knew the executor had to make such decisions,