      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me read through it carefully.\n\nThe context defines a virtual team as a community composed of two or more people across regions and organizations. They are connected through communication and information technology. They have complementary technologies or skills, common goals, common evaluation and work methods, and they jointly bear the final results and responsibilities.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options A, B, C, or D belongs to a virtual team based on the given definition.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: A publishing firm contacted several experts to jointly compile a new textbook. They work in different units and coordinating their work is mainly through telephone, fax, and network.\n\nHmm, so the experts are from different units, which implies different regions or organizations. They're using communication tools like telephone, fax, and network (which I assume is the internet). They have common goals (compiling a textbook) and are working together, possibly with complementary skills. They might be sharing responsibilities and results. This seems to fit the definition of a virtual team because they're connected through IT, from different regions, with common goals and methods.\n\nOption B: A company transferred each two key employees from the sales department, R&D department, and production department to form a new product research team.\n\nThis sounds like a team formed within the same company. The employees are moved from different departments, but they're still part of the same organization. The question is whether this counts as a virtual team. The definition says they should be across regions and organizations. If the company is a single organization, even if they're from different departments, they might not be considered across regions. So maybe this isn't a virtual team.\n\nOption C: Xiao Li encountered technical problems in his work. He was not familiar with a professional field. He consulted many experts through the internet and telephone, and established regular and fixed contacts with the experts.\n\nThis seems like Xiao Li is reaching out to experts for help. The experts are contacted via internet and phone, which are IT-based. However, the question is whether Xiao Li and the experts form a virtual team. The definition requires a community of two or more people, common goals, etc. Here, Xiao Li is consulting experts, but it's not clear if they are working together towards a common goal as a team. It might be more of a consulting relationship rather than a team.\n\nOption D: Xiao Tang, Xiao Sun, and Xiao Liu often chat on the internet and find that they have similar experiences and common hobbies. So they become good friends.\n\nThis is about three people becoming friends through internet chats. It doesn't mention anything about work, common goals, or complementary skills. It's more about personal relationships rather than a professional or work-related team. So this doesn't fit the definition of a virtual team.\n\nSo, evaluating each option:\n\nA fits because the experts are from different units (regions and possibly organizations), using IT for communication, with common goals, and working together.\n\nB might not fit because they're all from the same company, unless the departments are in different regions. The question doesn't specify, so I'm leaning towards B not being a virtual team.\n\nC is more about consulting rather than a team.\n\nD is personal friendship, not a work team.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be A.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is A.\n\n**Answer: A**",
