      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me read it carefully.\n\nThe context says that there are only liberal arts and science classes in a high school. So, the school offers two types of classes: liberal arts and science. No other types mentioned.\n\nNext, it states that there are more boys than girls. So, the total number of boys in the school is higher than the total number of girls.\n\nAlso, it mentions that there are more science classes than liberal arts classes. So, if I think in terms of numbers, the number of science classes is greater than the number of liberal arts classes. But wait, does that mean the number of students in science classes is more than in liberal arts? Or just the number of classes? Hmm, the wording says \"more science classes than liberal arts classes,\" so I think it refers to the count of classes, not the number of students. So, there are more science classes than liberal arts classes, but each class could have a different number of students.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options must be correct based on the given conditions. Let's look at the options:\n\nA) The total number of boys in the liberal arts class is more than the total number of girls in the liberal arts class.\n\nB) The total number of boys in the science class is more than the total number of girls in the science class.\n\nC) The total number of boys in the liberal arts class is higher than that of girls in the science class.\n\nD) The total number of boys in science class is more than that of girls in liberal arts class.\n\nWait, I need to parse each option carefully.\n\nFirst, let's consider the given information:\n\n- More boys than girls overall. So, total boys > total girls.\n\n- More science classes than liberal arts classes. So, number of science classes > number of liberal arts classes.\n\nBut we don't know how the students are distributed between the classes. So, for example, we don't know how many boys and girls are in each class, except that overall, boys are more than girls.\n\nLet me think about each option.\n\nOption A: Total boys in liberal arts > total girls in liberal arts.\n\nIs this necessarily true? Well, we know that overall, boys are more than girls, but that doesn't mean that in each class, boys outnumber girls. It's possible that in the liberal arts class, there are more girls than boys, but since there are more boys overall, they must be concentrated in the science classes. So, A might not necessarily be true.\n\nOption B: Total boys in science > total girls in science.\n\nSimilarly, since there are more boys overall, and more science classes, it's possible that the boys are concentrated in science classes. So, in each science class, there might be more boys than girls. But again, this isn't necessarily the case. It depends on how the students are distributed. For example, maybe all the boys are in science classes, but each science class could have more boys than girls. Or maybe some classes have more boys, others have more girls. But wait, the question is about the total number of boys in science vs. total girls in science. So, if the total boys are more than total girls, and all boys are in science, then yes, B would be true. But if some boys are in liberal arts, then it's possible that the total boys in science could be less than total girls in science. Wait, no, because overall boys are more than girls. So, if some boys are in liberal arts, then the number of boys in science would still be more than the number of girls in science. Let me think.\n\nWait, let's model this.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nTotal boys = B\n\nTotal girls = G\n\nGiven that B > G.\n\nNumber of science classes = S\n\nNumber of liberal arts classes = L\n\nGiven that S > L.\n\nNow, the number of students in each class: Let's say each class has a certain number of boys and girls. But we don't know the distribution.\n\nOption B: Total boys in science > total girls in science.\n\nSo, total boys in science = B_s\n\nTotal girls in science = G_s\n\nWe need to see if B_s > G_s must be true.\n\nSince B = B_s + B_la (boys in liberal arts)\n\nG = G_s + G_la (girls in liberal arts)\n\nGiven that B > G, so B_s + B_la > G_s + G_la.\n\nWe need to see if B_s > G_s.\n\nIs this necessarily true?\n\nSuppose that in science classes, the number of boys is equal to the number of girls. Then, B_s = G_s.\n\nBut then, since B > G, B_la must be greater than G_la.\n\nBut that doesn't necessarily mean that B_s > G_s. It could be equal.\n\nWait, but the question is whether B_s > G_s must be true.\n\nAlternatively, suppose that in science classes, there are more girls than boys. Then, B_s < G_s.\n\nBut since B > G, the boys must be concentrated elsewhere, i.e., in liberal arts. So, B_la would be greater than G_la.\n\nBut that doesn't necessarily mean that B_s > G_s.\n\nWait, let's take an example.\n\nSuppose total boys = 100, total girls = 80.\n\nNumber of science classes = 5, liberal arts = 3.\n\nNow, suppose that in each science class, there are 20 boys and 20 girls. So, total boys in science = 5*20=100, girls in science=5*20=100.\n\nBut wait, total boys are 100, so B_la=0, which can't be because there are liberal arts classes. So, that's not possible.\n\nWait, let me adjust.\n\nSuppose that in each science class, there are 15 boys and 10 girls. So, 5 classes: 75 boys and 50 girls in science.\n\nThen, total boys in science =75, total girls in science=50.\n\nTotal boys B=75 + B_la.\n\nTotal girls G=50 + G_la.\n\nGiven that B > G, so 75 + B_la > 50 + G_la.\n\nBut we don't know B_la and G_la. Suppose that in liberal arts, there are 25 boys and 30 girls.\n\nSo, total boys B=75+25=100, total girls G=50+30=80.\n\nSo, B=100, G=80, which satisfies B>G.\n\nIn this case, B_s=75 > G_s=50, so option B is true.\n\nBut is this the only possibility?\n\nSuppose that in science classes, there are 50 boys and 50 girls.\n\nSo, total boys in science=50, girls=50.\n\nThen, total boys B=50 + B