r Revenue Sources exist or not.\n\nWait, but the problem says the airport was unable to pay its operating expenses because the revenue from user fees was lower than expected. So, the only revenue mentioned is user fees, which were insufficient. Therefore, the total revenue (including other sources) was still less than expenses. So, the operating expenses were greater than the total revenue, including other sources. But D is comparing to other sources only, not the total. So, if other sources existed, but their revenue plus user fees was still less than expenses, then D would be incorrect because the expenses were greater than the other sources plus user fees, but D only compares to other sources.\n\nWait, no, D says that the operating expenses were greater than the revenue from other sources. So, if other sources existed, and their revenue plus user fees was still less than expenses, then D would be true because the expenses were greater than the other sources (since user fees were part of the total revenue, but D is only comparing to other sources). Wait, no, if other sources existed, and their revenue plus user fees was less than expenses, then D would be saying that expenses were greater than other sources, which would be true because even if other sources had some revenue, the total (including user fees) was still less than expenses. So, D would be true because the expenses were greater than the other sources (since the total was less than expenses, and other sources plus user fees were less than expenses, so other sources alone would be less than expenses).\n\nWait, I'm getting tangled here. Let me think differently.\n\nIf the only revenue was from user fees, then D is true because other sources would be zero, and expenses > 0.\n\nIf there were other sources, then the total revenue (user fees + other) was still less than expenses. So, the other sources plus user fees were less than expenses. Therefore, the other sources alone would be less than expenses, making